WEBVTT

00:00:00.115 --> 00:00:06.322
In Microsoft Fabric, a common workflow uses a Lakehouse, a notebook, Spark SQL, and Delta tables.

00:00:06.322 --> 00:00:14.137
You might upload or connect files, read them with Spark, create temporary views, transform data using SQL,

00:00:14.137 --> 00:00:15.552
and save curated results as tables.

00:00:15.552 --> 00:00:20.672
The same relational ideas still apply, but the environment is lakehouse-oriented.

00:00:20.672 --> 00:00:27.967
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:27.967 --> 00:00:34.172
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:34.172 --> 00:00:35.937
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:35.937 --> 00:00:41.172
In a real setting such as a retail pipeline aggregating high-volume sales data across partitions,

00:00:41.172 --> 00:00:48.727
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:48.727 --> 00:00:51.722
before discussing syntax.

00:00:51.722 --> 00:00:58.238
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
